{
	title: 'Generated heavy home for performance runs',
	spec: "src/perf/home.ts (perfHome.generate; CLI: bun src/perf/home.ts <home> [scale] [seed]) writes a Hal home shaped like a heavy user's, from filler text only: no real session data is read or committed. The shape is picked by hand and held in perfHome.shape: 60 open tabs (1 of 50 MB history, 2 of 20 MB, 5 of 5 MB, 12 of 1 MB, 40 of 200 kB) and 2,000 closed sessions of about 20 kB. Turns hold a prompt, rounds of thinking, markdown replies (lists, code fences, tables) and tool calls whose outputs are mostly short but sometimes thousands of lines, a few with lines of thousands of columns, ANSI color or wide characters. Open tabs are shuffled, except that one of the biggest comes first, so a client started in the sessions' cwd focuses it. A seeded PRNG makes the same seed and scale give byte-identical files; scale shrinks counts and sizes for quick runs. Histories are valid: numbered from 1, every tool call has its result (replay adds no missing-result notes), each ends with turn_end, so a host continues nothing. Generating the full home takes about 4 s and 500 MB.",
